Q1) Explain about different Levels of Testing.

    Testing Levels comprises of 4 levels and they are :
    -> Unit Testing
    -> Integration Testing
    -> System Testing 
    -> Acceptance Testing 

Unit Testing - 
             -> Unit testing tests the smallest testable part of an application independently.
             -> A unit is usually a:
                                  Function
                                  Method
                                  Class
                                  Small module
             -> The main goal is to verify that each individual component works correctly by itself.

Integration Testing - 
             -> Integration testing verifies that two or more components work correctly together.
             -> Even if individual units work perfectly, problems can occur when they communicate with each other.
             -> Ex: Consider an e-commerce application:
                   Order Service → Payment Service → Database
                Individually:
                             Order Service works.
                             Payment Service works.
                             Database works.

System Testing -
             -> System testing tests the complete, integrated application as a whole.
             -> We want to determine whether the entire system satisfies its specified requirements.
             -> Ex: For an e-commerce website, system testing might test the complete flow:
                   Login
                     ↓
               Search Product
                     ↓
                Add to Cart
                     ↓
                  Checkout
                     ↓
               Enter Address
                     ↓
               Make Payment
                     ↓
             Order Confirmation
                     ↓
           Email/SMS Notification

Acceptance Testing -
            -> Acceptance testing determines whether the system is acceptable to the customer/business/user and is ready for release or deployment.
            -> It is generally performed from a business/user perspective, rather than focusing on internal implementation details.4
            -> Ex: Suppose a company requested an e-commerce system with this requirement:
                   "A customer should be able to purchase a product using a credit card and receive an order confirmation."
               Acceptance testing verifies that the delivered system actually satisfies this business requirement.

Q2) Explain about Requirement Analysis.

Requirement Analysis is the systematic process of identifying, understanding, documenting, validating, and managing the needs and expectations of
stakeholders for a software system. Here they are categorised into 6 types and they are:
           -> Elicitation
           -> Analysis
           -> Evaluationa and Synthesis 
           -> Specification
           -> Validation
           -> Management

Elicitation - 
           -> Elicitation means collecting requirements from stakeholders.
           -> The goal is to understand what the customer/user actually needs.

Analysis - 
           -> Raw information gathered during elicitation is broken down, categorized, and examined in detail to understand its feasibility and impact.
           -> Identify gaps, ambiguities, missing information, technical constraints, and contradictory demands across different stakeholder groups.

Evaluation and Synthesis -
           -> Here, the requirements are evaluated, prioritized, and combined into a consistent set of system requirements.
           -> Combine fragmented needs into unified models and evaluate different implementation approaches.

Specification - 
           -> Specification translates evaluated concepts into a formal, structured document that acts as an agreement between clients,
              product managers, developers, and QA engineers.
           -> Document requirements precisely with clear acceptance criteria.

Validation - 
           -> Validation checks whether the documented requirements are correct and acceptable to the stakeholders.
           -> Ensure the requirements are complete, consistent, unambiguous, verifiable, and achievable.

Management - 
           -> Requirement Management deals with controlling and tracking requirements throughout the software development life cycle.
           -> Track, version, and control changes systematically.
